WB.L("snail-in-the-bottle", "society", "beginner", "The snail in the bottle: how negligence law was born",
  "A woman in a Scottish café sipped a ginger beer and claimed she found a rotting snail in it. Her lawsuit changed the law for almost everyone in the English-speaking world.",
  [
    ["A snail and no contract", "A woman who said she found a snail in her ginger beer had no contract with anyone, so the old law seemed to leave her without a remedy.",
      "On 26 August 1928, May Donoghue drank ginger beer from a dark, opaque bottle at the Wellmeadow Café in Paisley, near Glasgow. A friend had bought it. When the rest was poured into her ice cream float, she later said, the decomposed remains of a snail slid into her glass. She claimed shock and gastroenteritis.\n\nThe trouble was legal. If a faulty product hurt you, your main route was usually to sue the seller for breach of contract. She had no contract with the café owner, who had neither made the drink nor sealed the bottle. She had none with the manufacturer, David Stevenson, either. Earlier cases suggested that a manufacturer owed nothing to a stranger, except for inherently dangerous items."],
    ["Up to the Lords", "The Lords decided only whether she had a claim in law, not whether the snail was real.",
      "Mrs Donoghue had little money, so she was allowed to sue as a pauper, without paying the usual court costs. Her lawyer, Walter Leechman, took on the manufacturer. The case reached the House of Lords, then Britain's highest court, which ruled in May 1932.\n\nThe judges did not decide whether a snail had really been in the bottle. They answered a preliminary question: assuming everything she said was true, did the law give her a claim? By three votes to two, they said yes. A manufacturer who sells a product meant to reach the consumer unchanged owes that consumer a duty to take reasonable care."],
    ["Who is my neighbour?", "Lord Atkin's neighbour principle says you owe reasonable care to anyone you can foresee your actions might injure.",
      "The most famous judgment came from Lord Atkin, who reached for the parable of the Good Samaritan, in which a lawyer asks, 'Who is my neighbour?' Atkin gave a legal answer. You must take reasonable care to avoid acts or omissions that you can reasonably foresee would be likely to injure your neighbour. And your neighbour is anyone so closely and directly affected by what you do that you ought to have them in mind.\n\nThis was a broad principle, not a narrow rule about ginger beer. A duty of care can exist between strangers wherever harm is foreseeable and the relationship is close enough. Lawyers call it the neighbour principle, and first-year law students still learn it."],
    ["What negligence requires", "To win a negligence claim you generally must show a duty of care, a breach of that duty, and harm caused by the breach.",
      "Donoghue v Stevenson gave shape to negligence, the law of careless harm. Broadly, a person suing must show three things. First, the other side owed them a duty of care. Second, the other side breached it by falling below the standard of a reasonable person in their position. Third, the breach actually caused harm of a kind the law recognises.\n\nThese ideas now sit behind claims about car crashes, medical mistakes, slippery supermarket floors and unsafe buildings. Courts have spent decades refining where duties begin and end, because a duty that stretched forever would make everyone liable for everything. Many places, including Australian states, have also passed laws limiting some claims and damages."],
    ["The snail travels", "The principle spread across the common law world, though no court ever decided whether the snail existed.",
      "A Scottish decision about a Paisley café could have stayed local. Instead, in the mid-1930s, an Australian case reached the Privy Council in London, then Australia's final court of appeal. Dr Richard Grant had developed severe dermatitis from woollen underwear made by Australian Knitting Mills, which contained irritating chemicals left from manufacturing. Relying on Donoghue v Stevenson, the Privy Council held the manufacturer liable. Courts in Canada and New Zealand followed, and governments later built consumer protection laws on these foundations.\n\nHere is the twist. David Stevenson died before the trial on the facts, and the claim was settled out of court. No judge ever ruled on whether the snail existed. A memorial now marks the café site."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Mrs Donoghue seem to have no legal claim?", ["Snails in drinks were not considered harmful by the courts","She had no contract with the seller or the maker of the drink","The café had closed before she could bring a legal action","Ginger beer was exempt from the product laws of the time"], 1, "Under the old law, a stranger who used a faulty product could not sue the maker."],
    [1, "What did the House of Lords decide?", ["That a snail had really been in the bottle, so she won","That only the café owner could be sued for the harm","If her story was true, manufacturers owed her reasonable care","That she must wait for Parliament to pass a new product law"], 2, "The Lords answered a question of law, not of fact, and created a duty of care for manufacturers."],
    [2, "What does the neighbour principle say?", ["You owe care only to people you have a contract with","You owe care only to family and close friends","You owe care only to people who are paying you","You owe care to anyone you can foresee you might harm"], 3, "Duty of care extends to anyone so closely affected that you ought to have them in mind."],
    [3, "What must you generally show to win a negligence claim?", ["A duty of care, its breach, and resulting harm","A contract, a payment and a written complaint","Intent, motive and a full confession","A witness, a receipt and a medical report"], 0, "Negligence needs a duty, a failure to meet the reasonable standard, and resulting harm."],
    [4, "Why do we not know whether the snail was real?", ["The bottle was lost before the trial could take place","The Lords ruled that the snail had never existed at all","The maker died and the claim was settled out of court","The jury could not reach a verdict on the evidence given"], 2, "The case is remembered for its legal principle, not its facts."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A woman, a bottle and a possibly imaginary snail changed the law."});

WB.L("habeas-corpus", "society", "beginner", "Habeas corpus: the right not to simply vanish",
  "Two Latin words protect you from being locked away without reason. They have stood up to kings, slave owners and presidents.",
  [
    ["Produce the body", "Habeas corpus is a court order demanding that a jailer justify why a person is being held.",
      "Habeas corpus is Latin for 'you shall have the body'. It is the name of a writ, a formal court order, directed at whoever is holding a prisoner, whether a jailer, a soldier or a government official. The writ commands them to bring the person before the court and show a lawful reason for the detention. If they cannot, the judge can order the prisoner released.\n\nIt does not decide whether someone is guilty. It asks a more basic question: is there any legal authority to hold this person at all? That makes it a check on power itself. Without it, a government could make people disappear into cells with no charge, no trial and no end date."],
    ["Forged by conflict", "English courts and Parliament turned the writ into a real check on the Crown, ending with the Habeas Corpus Act of 1679.",
      "Versions of the writ existed in English courts by the 1200s, at first mostly to bring people to court. Judges later used it to test whether detentions were lawful, drawing strength from Magna Carta of 1215: no free man to be imprisoned except by the lawful judgment of his peers or the law of the land.\n\nIn 1627 Charles I jailed knights who refused a forced loan demanded without Parliament's consent. The jailer's only answer was that they were held by the king's special command, and the court refused to release them. Outrage helped produce the Petition of Right in 1628, declaring such imprisonment unlawful. In 1679 Parliament passed the Habeas Corpus Act, setting deadlines and penalties to stop officials dodging the writ."],
    ["James Somerset's freedom", "In 1772 the writ stopped an enslaved man being shipped away by force, a landmark in the struggle against slavery.",
      "In 1772 the writ was used in a dramatic case. James Somerset had been enslaved in Virginia and brought to England by Charles Stewart, a customs official. Somerset escaped, was recaptured and was put aboard a ship bound for Jamaica, where he would be sold.\n\nSupporters, including the campaigner Granville Sharp, sought habeas corpus. Lord Mansfield, the senior judge of the King's Bench, ruled that no law in England allowed a master to force Somerset out of the country, and Somerset was freed. The decision did not abolish slavery, which continued in British colonies for decades. But it was widely celebrated and became a landmark in the long struggle against slavery."],
    ["Suspending the writ", "Governments in crisis have suspended habeas corpus, and each time it has been controversial.",
      "Because habeas corpus limits government power, governments under pressure have sometimes tried to switch it off. Britain's Parliament suspended it several times, for example in the 1790s amid fears of revolution spreading from France.\n\nThe framers of the United States Constitution included the writ but allowed suspension only in cases of rebellion or invasion when public safety required it. In 1861, at the start of the Civil War, President Abraham Lincoln suspended it in some areas. Chief Justice Roger Taney argued in the Merryman case that only Congress had that power, and Lincoln largely ignored him. Congress later authorised suspension. Lawyers and historians still debate how far emergencies justify setting aside this ancient safeguard."],
    ["The writ today", "Habeas corpus remains part of the law in Australia and across the common law world, and matters most when other protections fail.",
      "English colonists carried habeas corpus around the world. It remains part of the law in countries including Australia, New Zealand, Canada, India and the United States. In Australia, superior courts such as the state Supreme Courts and the High Court can issue it.\n\nIn practice, many detentions are now reviewed through other routes, such as bail hearings, appeals and judicial review, so the old writ is used less often than its fame suggests. But it still matters most when other protections fail. It has been invoked over immigration detention, mental health detention and wartime imprisonment. Its message endures: power must always answer one question in court. By what lawful authority is this person being held?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What question does habeas corpus force a jailer to answer?", ["Whether the prisoner is guilty or innocent","What lawful authority justifies the detention","How long the sentence should be served","Who first reported the crime to police"], 1, "The writ tests the legality of the detention, not guilt or innocence."],
    [1, "How did the 1679 Habeas Corpus Act strengthen the writ?", ["It abolished the Crown's power to arrest anyone","It limited the writ to use by nobles only","It set deadlines and penalties for officials","It replaced the writ with trial by jury"], 2, "Parliament added deadlines and penalties so officials could not simply ignore the court."],
    [2, "What did the Somerset case show?", ["It stopped a forced removal from England, though slavery continued","It abolished slavery across the British Empire in the year 1772","It showed that judges could not interfere with ship captains","It showed the writ applied only to British-born subjects"], 0, "Habeas corpus freed Somerset, and the decision became a landmark in the struggle against slavery."],
    [3, "Why is suspending habeas corpus so controversial?", ["It makes courts hear far more cases than they can handle","It lengthens the sentences of prisoners already convicted","It removes the writ only for visitors from foreign countries","It lets authorities hold people without justifying it to a court"], 3, "The writ is a check on government power, so switching it off removes that check."],
    [4, "Why is the writ used less often today?", ["Most countries have formally abolished it in recent years","Governments have permanently suspended it in peacetime","Other routes review many detentions, but it backs them up","It can only ever be used during a declared war"], 2, "Bail, appeals and judicial review handle most cases, leaving habeas corpus as the safeguard of last resort."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Two Latin words stand between you and a cell with no end date. What do they demand?"});

WB.L("referendum-1967", "society", "beginner", "The 1967 referendum: what it really changed",
  "More than 90 per cent of Australians voted Yes in 1967, the biggest win in referendum history. Yet many people still believe it did things it never did.",
  [
    ["The biggest Yes ever", "The 1967 referendum won about 91 per cent of the vote after a decade of campaigning by Aboriginal and non-Aboriginal Australians.",
      "On 27 May 1967, about 91 per cent of Australians voted Yes to a question about Aboriginal people, and every state agreed. It is the biggest Yes in referendum history, in a country where change is hard. A proposal needs a majority of voters nationally and majorities in at least four of six states, and only a handful of more than 40 proposals have passed.\n\nThe result came from a decade of campaigning. The Federal Council for the Advancement of Aborigines and Torres Strait Islanders gathered petitions, with leaders including Faith Bandler and Joe McGinness. The 1965 Freedom Ride, led by Charles Perkins and students, exposed segregation in country New South Wales. By the vote, the major parties backed Yes and there was almost no organised No case."],
    ["Two changes only", "The referendum deleted a phrase from the race power and removed section 127, and those two changes were its entire legal effect.",
      "The 1901 Constitution mentioned Aboriginal people only twice, and both times to exclude them. Section 51 lists the subjects the federal Parliament can make laws about. It gave power over 'the people of any race, other than the aboriginal race in any State', which left laws about Aboriginal people to the states. Section 127 said that in reckoning the numbers of the people of the Commonwealth, 'aboriginal natives shall not be counted'.\n\nThe 1967 referendum removed the words 'other than the aboriginal race in any State' from section 51, and deleted section 127 altogether. That was the entire legal effect of the vote."],
    ["Myth: the right to vote", "The referendum did not give Aboriginal people the vote, because voting rights come from ordinary laws passed earlier.",
      "One of the most common beliefs about 1967 is that it gave Aboriginal people the right to vote. It did not. Voting rights are set mainly by ordinary laws passed by parliaments, not by the Constitution.\n\nSome Aboriginal people could vote in a few colonies from the 1850s and 1890s, although many were later excluded. In 1962 the Commonwealth Electoral Act was amended to give all Aboriginal people the right to vote in federal elections. The states moved at different speeds, and Queensland was the last to grant state voting rights, in 1965. So by the referendum, Aboriginal Australians had the right to vote everywhere, even though many still faced real barriers in practice."],
    ["Myths: citizenship and fauna", "The vote did not create citizenship, and no law ever classed Aboriginal people as flora and fauna.",
      "Another myth is that the referendum made Aboriginal people citizens. Australian citizenship was created in 1949, and Aboriginal people born in Australia were covered by it. In practice, many were still denied rights by state laws controlling where they lived, worked and married.\n\nA third myth says Aboriginal people were managed under a 'Flora and Fauna Act' until 1967. Historians and fact checkers have found no such law, at federal or state level. The myth probably spread because it captured how dehumanising the real treatment felt, but it is not accurate. Correcting these myths does not shrink the referendum's importance. It shows that its power was partly symbolic, and partly about which government held responsibility."],
    ["What it did change", "The referendum let the federal Parliament make laws for Aboriginal people, but progress came more slowly than many hoped.",
      "Removing the exclusion from section 51 meant the Commonwealth, not just the states, could now make laws specifically for Aboriginal people. The federal government went on to create new agencies and programs, and in 1976 Parliament passed the Aboriginal Land Rights (Northern Territory) Act. Deleting section 127 meant Aboriginal people would be included in official population counts.\n\nYet the change was neutral on its face. The race power covers people of any race, and courts have since debated whether laws made under it must benefit the group. Many campaigners felt real progress came more slowly than the 1967 hope promised. Even so, the vote remains a landmark, and its date now marks the start of National Reconciliation Week."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What made the 1967 result so unusual?", ["It was the first referendum ever held in Australia","It passed by only a single vote in a close national count","About 91 per cent voted Yes and every state agreed","The major parties campaigned hard against the proposal"], 2, "Years of campaigning and broad political support produced the biggest Yes in referendum history."],
    [1, "What did the referendum change in the Constitution?", ["It deleted words in the race power and removed section 127","It gave Aboriginal people the right to vote in federal elections","It created Australian citizenship for Aboriginal people","It returned all Indigenous affairs to the state governments"], 0, "Those two changes were the entire legal effect of the vote."],
    [2, "Why is 'the 1967 referendum gave Aboriginal people the vote' a myth?", ["Aboriginal people were never allowed to vote until 1975","The Constitution already gave all adults the vote","Only Aboriginal men gained the vote in 1967","Earlier ordinary laws had already granted the vote"], 3, "Federal voting rights came in 1962 and Queensland followed in 1965, before the referendum."],
    [3, "What is true of the claim that a 'Flora and Fauna Act' governed Aboriginal people?", ["It was repealed in 1967","It applied only in Queensland","No such law has ever been found","It was replaced by 1949 citizenship laws"], 2, "The myth captures how dehumanising treatment felt, but no such law existed."],
    [4, "What new power did the change give the federal Parliament?", ["To abolish the state parliaments and govern directly","To grant Australian citizenship to Aboriginal people","To make laws specifically for Aboriginal people, not only the states","To set the national voting age for all federal elections"], 2, "Removing the exclusion from section 51 opened a new era of national policy."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The biggest Yes in Australian history. What many people wrongly think it did."});

WB.L("gerrymandering", "society", "beginner", "Gerrymandering: winning by drawing the lines",
  "You can lose the popular vote and still win the election, if someone has drawn the map cleverly enough. Meet the strange art of the gerrymander.",
  [
    ["A salamander in Massachusetts", "The word 'gerrymander' comes from an 1812 cartoon mocking a district shaped like a monster.",
      "In 1812 the Massachusetts legislature redrew its state senate districts to help the governing party, and Governor Elbridge Gerry signed the map into law. One district north of Boston twisted so strangely that critics said it looked like a salamander. The Boston Gazette published a cartoon turning it into a winged, clawed beast and named it the 'Gerry-mander'.\n\nThe insult stuck. Gerry's name was pronounced with a hard G, like Gary, but the word is now almost always said with a soft G, like Jerry. Gerry himself reportedly disliked the plan, yet his name is forever attached to it."],
    ["Packing and cracking", "Gerrymanders work by packing opponents into a few seats or cracking them thinly across many.",
      "Where each electorate elects one member, boundaries matter enormously. Imagine a region with ten seats, where two parties each have half the voters. Draw the lines one way and each party wins five seats. Draw them another way and one party can win seven or eight.\n\nThere are two classic tricks. Packing means cramming as many of your opponent's supporters as possible into a few seats, which they win by huge, wasteful margins. Cracking means splitting the rest thinly across many seats, so they fall just short everywhere. Their votes are not taken away. They are arranged so that many make no difference. With modern data and computers, these techniques can be applied with remarkable precision."],
    ["Unequal seats", "When seats hold unequal numbers of voters, some votes count for more, and Australia has its own examples.",
      "A cousin of the gerrymander is malapportionment, where some electorates hold many more voters than others. If country seats have half the voters of city seats, each country vote counts roughly double. It sometimes reflected a belief that rural areas needed extra representation, and sometimes it simply helped whoever drew the map. Britain's 'rotten boroughs' were tiny constituencies with a handful of voters that still elected MPs, until reforms from 1832 began to sweep them away.\n\nIn South Australia, heavily weighted rural seats kept Thomas Playford's government in power for decades, a system nicknamed the Playmander. Queensland's zonal system, introduced under Labor in 1949 and later reshaped by Country and National Party governments, was nicknamed the Bjelkemander after Premier Joh Bjelke-Petersen. Both let parties win government with fewer votes than their rivals."],
    ["Drawing lines fairly", "Australia moved boundary drawing to independent bodies, with electorates of roughly equal size.",
      "After the Fitzgerald Inquiry into corruption in the late 1980s, Queensland moved to boundaries drawn by an independent commission with roughly equal enrolments. Other states have also moved towards equal weighting over time.\n\nToday federal boundaries are drawn by the Australian Electoral Commission, an independent statutory body, not by politicians. When population shifts or a state's number of seats changes, a redistribution is held. Committees propose boundaries, the public and parties can lodge objections, and an independent commission makes the final decision. The law requires electorates in a state to have close to the same number of voters, within set tolerances, while also considering communities of interest, transport links and physical features. States and territories run similar independent processes. Parties still argue about decisions, but the pencil is out of the hands of those who benefit."],
    ["The fight elsewhere", "In many US states legislators still draw the lines, and federal courts will not rule on partisan gerrymanders.",
      "In the United States, district lines are usually drawn by state legislatures, so whichever party controls a state can shape its map. Both major parties have been accused of gerrymandering where they hold power. Some states have handed the job to independent or bipartisan commissions, often after citizen ballot initiatives.\n\nUS Supreme Court decisions in the 1960s, especially Reynolds v Sims in 1964, required state legislative districts to have roughly equal populations, a principle known as one person, one vote. Courts also hear challenges over racial discrimination. But in 2019, in Rucho v Common Cause, the Court held that partisan gerrymandering claims are political questions federal courts cannot resolve. Researchers use measures such as the efficiency gap, which counts wasted votes, to detect unfair maps."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a gerrymander?", ["Drawing electoral boundaries to favour one party","Counting votes in a different order","Giving some voters two ballots","Changing the voting age to suit a party"], 0, "The name comes from a district drawn to help one party win."],
    [1, "What is 'cracking'?", ["Cramming opponents into a few seats they win by huge margins","Removing opponents from the electoral roll","Spreading opponents thinly across many seats so they fall short","Redrawing every seat after each election"], 2, "Cracking wastes the opponent's votes by spreading them, while packing wastes them by concentrating them."],
    [2, "Why does malapportionment distort election results?", ["It changes the way the votes are counted afterwards","Votes in seats with fewer voters count for more","It lets voters choose which electorate they join","It removes some voters from the electoral roll"], 1, "If a country seat has half the voters of a city seat, each country vote counts about double."],
    [3, "Why are Australian federal boundaries considered fairer?", ["The governing party draws them and the High Court approves","Each state draws its own with no oversight at all","An independent commission draws them with near-equal voter numbers","They were fixed at Federation and have never changed"], 2, "Independent redistributions keep the pencil away from those who benefit."],
    [4, "Why are partisan maps still common in the United States?", ["State legislatures usually draw them, and courts avoid partisan claims","Federal law requires the major parties to draw them together","Independent commissions are banned in every single state","Courts reject all challenges, including racial discrimination"], 0, "Control of the legislature means control of the map, and the courts have left partisan claims to states and Congress."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A cartoon salamander gave us a word for winning by redrawing the map."});

WB.L("diplomatic-immunity", "society", "beginner", "Diplomatic immunity: why ambassadors can't be arrested",
  "A diplomat can park illegally, refuse to answer police and walk free from a courtroom. There is a surprisingly old and sensible reason why.",
  [
    ["Don't shoot the messenger", "Protecting envoys is one of the oldest rules of international relations, because without it no one could negotiate.",
      "Rulers have always needed to talk to their rivals, even during wars. That only works if messengers can travel safely. Ancient Greek heralds, Roman envoys and ambassadors in many other civilisations were protected, often with religious overtones.\n\nIn the early 1200s, Mongol envoys sent by Genghis Khan to the Khwarazmian Empire in Central Asia were mistreated, and at least one was executed. Genghis Khan responded with a devastating invasion from 1219. The lesson has been learned again and again. If envoys can be arrested, jailed or killed, diplomacy collapses, and the only remaining way to settle disputes is force."],
    ["An ambassador arrested", "Britain passed a law protecting diplomats after Russia's ambassador was dragged from his coach over debts.",
      "In 1708, Andrey Matveyev, the ambassador of Tsar Peter the Great, was about to leave London when he was dragged from his coach and briefly locked up over unpaid debts to local merchants. Peter was furious and demanded that the offenders be severely punished.\n\nThe British government was embarrassed, but under English law the creditors had done nothing illegal. To repair the relationship, Parliament passed the Diplomatic Privileges Act, which made it unlawful to arrest ambassadors or seize their goods, and Queen Anne's government sent a formal apology to Moscow. It shows how international law often grows: an awkward incident exposes a gap, and states create rules so it does not happen again."],
    ["The Vienna Convention", "Modern diplomatic immunity comes from a 1961 treaty almost every country has joined.",
      "The main rules are in the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations of 1961, one of the most widely accepted treaties in the world. Australia gives effect to it through its Diplomatic Privileges and Immunities Act of 1967.\n\nUnder the convention, diplomatic agents cannot be arrested or detained, and they are immune from the host country's criminal courts. They are also immune from most civil and administrative cases, with exceptions such as private property deals or business unrelated to their official work. Their families generally share these protections. The diplomatic bag, used to send documents and items between an embassy and its home government, must not be opened or detained. A separate, narrower 1963 convention protects consular officials."],
    ["Is an embassy foreign soil?", "An embassy remains part of the host country, but local officials cannot enter without the mission head's consent.",
      "Films often say that stepping into an embassy means stepping onto foreign soil. That is a myth. An embassy in Canberra stands on Australian territory, and in general Australian law applies there.\n\nWhat the Vienna Convention does is make the premises inviolable. Police and other officials of the host country may not enter without the consent of the head of the mission, even with a warrant, and the host country must protect the building from intrusion or damage. That is why people sometimes seek refuge inside embassies. They have not left the country, but local authorities cannot simply walk in and take them. The effect can look similar, but the legal reasoning is quite different."],
    ["Why keep immunity?", "Immunity is not impunity: abusers can be expelled, and every country gains from protecting envoys.",
      "Diplomats must respect local laws. When they break them, the host country can ask the sending country to waive immunity so the person can be prosecuted, or declare the diplomat persona non grata, an unwelcome person, who must then leave. In April 1984, shots fired from the Libyan embassy in London killed Police Constable Yvonne Fletcher during a protest. Britain could not enter or prosecute, so after a siege it expelled the diplomats and broke off relations.\n\nWhy keep the system? Reciprocity. Every country has diplomats abroad, sometimes in places with hostile governments. If a host could arrest them on real or invented charges, they could become hostages. Immunity lets envoys report honestly and deliver unwelcome messages without fear."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why have societies long protected envoys?", ["Envoys were always members of the royal family","Armies needed envoys to carry supplies to the front","Without safe messengers, rivals cannot negotiate","Rulers wanted to profit from the trade envoys carried"], 2, "Protecting envoys keeps channels open that can prevent war."],
    [1, "What does the arrest of Russia's ambassador in London show about how international law grows?", ["An awkward incident exposes a gap, then states make new rules","Treaties are always written before any incident happens","Only full-scale wars have ever created new international rules","Courts invent the rules without any state agreeing to them"], 0, "Parliament protected ambassadors by statute after the embarrassing arrest."],
    [2, "Under the Vienna Convention, what protection does a diplomat generally have?", ["Freedom from any obligation to respect local laws","Immunity from arrest and from criminal courts","Immunity from being expelled by the host country","The right to be tried only inside their own embassy"], 1, "Diplomats are immune from arrest and criminal courts, but they can still be expelled."],
    [3, "What is the legal status of an embassy?", ["It is foreign soil where only the sending country's laws apply","It is open to police who arrive carrying a warrant","It is host territory, but officials need the mission head's consent","It is owned and administered by the United Nations"], 2, "Inviolability, not foreign soil, is what keeps local authorities out."],
    [4, "Why do nearly all countries respect diplomatic immunity?", ["An international police force enforces the rules everywhere","They earn income from the trade that diplomats bring","Each state has envoys abroad it wants protected, so all gain","Diplomats pay a fee to their host countries for it"], 2, "Reciprocity makes the occasional injustice the price of keeping diplomacy safe."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A diplomat can ignore a police summons. The reason is older and wiser than it looks."});

WB.L("geneva-conventions", "society", "intermediate", "The Geneva Conventions: rules for the worst of times",
  "Even war has laws. They began with a Swiss businessman who stumbled onto a battlefield and could not forget what he saw.",
  [
    ["From Solferino to Geneva", "One businessman's horror at a battlefield led to the Red Cross and the first Geneva Convention.",
      "In June 1859, Swiss businessman Henry Dunant travelled to northern Italy hoping to meet Napoleon III about a business venture. He arrived just after the Battle of Solferino. Tens of thousands lay dead or wounded, with little medical help. Dunant organised local villagers, many of them women, to care for the wounded of both sides.\n\nHis 1862 book, A Memory of Solferino, proposed trained volunteer relief groups and an international agreement protecting the wounded and their carers. In 1863 he and four other Genevans formed the committee that became the International Committee of the Red Cross. In 1864 twelve states signed the first Geneva Convention. Wounded and sick soldiers had to be cared for on either side, and ambulances, hospitals and medical staff were protected, marked by a red cross on white, the reverse of the Swiss flag."],
    ["A late honour", "Dunant ended his life in poverty and obscurity, but was later awarded the first Nobel Peace Prize.",
      "Dunant's own story did not end happily for many years. His business ventures in Algeria failed, he went bankrupt in the 1860s, and the scandal forced him out of the organisation he had helped create. He drifted through Europe in poverty and eventually settled in the small Swiss town of Heiden, where he lived quietly in a hospital home.\n\nIn the 1890s a journalist rediscovered him, and his story spread again. In 1901 Dunant shared the very first Nobel Peace Prize with the French peace campaigner Frédéric Passy. He died in 1910. His birthday, 8 May, is now celebrated as World Red Cross and Red Crescent Day."],
    ["Four conventions", "After the Second World War, states agreed four new conventions in 1949, including one to protect civilians.",
      "The original treaty was updated in 1906 and 1929, but the Second World War exposed its limits. Millions of prisoners of war died in captivity, and civilians suffered mass killings, deportations and starvation under occupation.\n\nIn 1949 states agreed on four new Geneva Conventions. The first protects wounded and sick soldiers on land. The second does the same for those wounded, sick or shipwrecked at sea. The third sets out how prisoners of war must be treated, including humane conditions and protection from torture and public curiosity. The fourth, entirely new, protects civilians in enemy hands and in occupied territory. Every country in the world has ratified the 1949 conventions, which makes them truly universal."],
    ["The key principles", "Humanitarian law rests on distinction, proportionality and humane treatment, and applies equally to all sides.",
      "Two Additional Protocols in 1977 strengthened protections for civilians and for victims of civil wars. Together with other treaties, such as the Hague Conventions on weapons and tactics, they form international humanitarian law.\n\nA few principles run through it. Distinction means combatants must tell military targets from civilians, and may only target the former. Proportionality means an attack is unlawful if the expected harm to civilians would be excessive compared with the concrete military advantage. People who are no longer fighting, such as the wounded, the sick and those who surrender, must be treated humanely. These rules do not decide whether a war is justified. They apply equally to all sides, whatever the cause."],
    ["Emblems and enforcement", "Grave breaches are war crimes that any state must pursue, though enforcement remains the hardest part.",
      "The red cross emblem is protected by law and must not be misused, even for decoration or advertising. The red crescent has been used since the 1870s, and in 2005 states adopted a third emblem, the red crystal, with no religious or national associations.\n\nEnforcement is the hardest part. The conventions require states to search for and prosecute people accused of grave breaches, such as wilful killing, torture or taking hostages, whatever their nationality. Australia does this through its own legislation, including the Geneva Conventions Act 1957. Since the 1990s, international tribunals and the International Criminal Court have also tried war crimes. Violations still happen far too often, but the rules give victims a standard and the world a way to judge."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the first Geneva Convention require?", ["Prisoners of war to be released as soon as a battle ended","Wounded and sick soldiers to be cared for on either side","Armies to stop fighting at night and on holy days","Civilians to be moved away from every battlefield"], 1, "Neutrality and protection for the wounded and their carers was the revolutionary idea."],
    [1, "What happened to Dunant after founding the Red Cross?", ["He fell into poverty, but later won the first Nobel Peace Prize","He led the Red Cross for the rest of his long life","He became a wealthy banker in Geneva and a public figure","He was forgotten and never received any public honour"], 0, "Dunant's idea outlasted his own fortunes, and he was finally honoured in 1901."],
    [2, "What did the fourth 1949 convention add?", ["Rules covering only naval battles and ship crews","Limits on which weapons armies were allowed to build","Protection for civilians in enemy hands and occupied territory","Rules on the circumstances in which wars may begin"], 2, "The Second World War showed that civilians needed their own treaty."],
    [3, "What does the principle of proportionality mean?", ["Both sides must field equal numbers of troops","Civilians may be targeted if the cause is a just one","Civilian harm must not be excessive for the gain","Attacks are lawful whenever a senior general approves them"], 2, "Proportionality limits harm to civilians even when a target is legitimate."],
    [4, "Who can pursue people accused of grave breaches?", ["Every state must search for and prosecute them","Only the country where the breach occurred may do so","Only the Red Cross may bring such people to account","Only the accused person's own country may do so"], 0, "The conventions make grave breaches a matter for all states, not just the combatants."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A businessman went to meet an emperor and ended up writing rules for war."});

WB.L("lindy-chamberlain", "society", "intermediate", "Lindy Chamberlain and the dingo: a wrongful conviction",
  "A baby vanished from a tent near Uluru, and her mother was convicted of murder. It took decades, and a chance discovery, to prove the truth.",
  [
    ["A night at Uluru", "Azaria Chamberlain vanished from a tent in 1980, and public suspicion turned on her parents.",
      "On 17 August 1980, Michael and Lindy Chamberlain, a pastor and his wife from Mount Isa, were camping near Uluru. Their nine-week-old daughter Azaria was asleep in the tent. Lindy heard a cry, went to check and saw a dingo leaving. Azaria was gone. She was never found, though some of her clothing turned up near a dingo den a week later.\n\nMany Australians found the story hard to believe. Rumours spread about the family's Seventh-day Adventist faith, including the false claim that Azaria's name meant 'sacrifice in the wilderness'. Lindy's calm in public was read as coldness. A 1981 inquest found a dingo took the baby, but police kept investigating a murder theory."],
    ["The forensic case", "The prosecution relied on confident forensic evidence that was later shown to be badly flawed.",
      "A second inquest led to Lindy being charged with murder and Michael with being an accessory after the fact. There was no body, no confession, no clear motive and no witness to any killing. The case rested heavily on science. Experts testified that tests found foetal blood in the family's car, including a spray pattern under the dashboard. A forensic expert argued that marks on the jumpsuit looked like a bloody handprint and that its damage was made by a cutting instrument, not a dingo's teeth. The prosecution suggested Lindy had cut Azaria's throat in the car.\n\nThe Darwin jury, faced with confident experts, convicted. In October 1982 Lindy was sentenced to life imprisonment with hard labour, and Michael received a suspended sentence. Appeals, including to the High Court, failed."],
    ["A jacket in the sand", "In 1986 a missing piece of clothing was found by chance, and a Royal Commission dismantled the forensic evidence.",
      "Lindy had always said Azaria wore a small matinee jacket, which had never been found. In early 1986, an English tourist, David Brett, fell to his death while climbing Uluru. Searching for his remains, police found the jacket half buried near a dingo lair, in an area not easily reached. It supported Lindy's account. Within days she was released, having served more than three years.\n\nThe government set up a Royal Commission, led by Justice Trevor Morling. His 1987 report found serious problems with the forensic evidence. Most strikingly, the supposed spray of foetal blood under the dashboard was shown to be a sound deadening compound applied when the car was made."],
    ["Clearing their names", "The convictions were quashed in 1988, but a coroner only confirmed in 2012 that a dingo took Azaria.",
      "Morling concluded that the convictions could not be justified on the evidence. In September 1988 the Northern Territory Court of Criminal Appeal quashed the convictions of both Lindy and Michael. In 1992 the Chamberlains received compensation of about 1.3 million dollars for their wrongful imprisonment and legal costs.\n\nYet the official cause of death remained uncertain. A third inquest, in 1995, returned an open finding. Only at a fourth inquest, in June 2012, did the Northern Territory coroner, Elizabeth Morris, formally find that Azaria had been taken and killed by a dingo, and the death certificate was changed. More than 30 years after that night, the family's account was finally recognised in law."],
    ["What the case taught", "A conviction is only as strong as its evidence, and confident experts and public prejudice can mislead.",
      "The case is now studied as a warning about wrongful convictions. Experts presented uncertain results with too much confidence, and some tests were poorly done or misread. The jury had little way to know.\n\nIn the years that followed, Australian forensic science moved towards stricter standards, accreditation and more careful presentation of evidence in court. The case also showed how media coverage and public prejudice can shape a trial, as people judged Lindy by her manner and religion rather than the facts. Later dingo attacks on children, including a fatal attack on Fraser Island, now K'gari, in 2001, ended any lingering doubt that dingoes can be dangerous. Above all, a conviction is only as strong as its evidence."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What fed public suspicion of the Chamberlains?", ["A confession Lindy gave to police early on","Rumours about their faith and Lindy's calm manner","Evidence that Azaria had been seen alive later","A clear motive uncovered by the investigators"], 1, "People judged Lindy by her manner and faith rather than by the facts."],
    [1, "What did the prosecution's case rest on?", ["Several eyewitnesses who saw the killing take place","A signed confession from Michael to the police","Forensic evidence that experts presented confidently","Proof that dingoes never enter campgrounds near Uluru"], 2, "Jurors trusted confident experts, whose results were later shown to be flawed."],
    [2, "What did the jacket and the Royal Commission reveal?", ["The jacket backed Lindy; the 'blood' was car sound deadener","The jacket proved Lindy had been lying to police all along","The blood tests were correct but the jury misread them","The dashboard stain was confirmed to come from the baby"], 0, "New evidence and a review showed the forensic case could not stand."],
    [3, "Why did the coroner only confirm a dingo took Azaria in 2012?", ["Earlier findings left the cause of death unsettled or open","The family never asked for any further inquest until 2012","The jacket was lost again and no new evidence emerged","Police refused to reopen the case until the year 2012"], 0, "Clearing the Chamberlains came in stages, and the cause of death was formally settled last."],
    [4, "What is the main lesson of the case?", ["Confident experts can mislead, so evidence needs scrutiny","Dingoes are harmless to children, as the case proved","Juries should never be allowed to hear forensic evidence","Media coverage cannot influence the outcome of a trial"], 0, "The case pushed Australian forensic science towards stricter standards and more careful evidence."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Australia convicted a mother over a missing baby. Then a jacket turned up."});

WB.L("marbury-v-madison", "society", "intermediate", "Marbury v Madison: how courts got the power to strike down laws",
  "A row over an undelivered job letter gave judges one of the greatest powers in modern government. The judge who did it managed to win by appearing to lose.",
  [
    ["The midnight commission", "An undelivered job letter from the outgoing president took William Marbury straight to the Supreme Court.",
      "John Adams and the Federalists lost the 1800 election to Thomas Jefferson. Before handing over in March 1801, Adams created new judicial posts and filled them with Federalists, whom critics called the midnight judges. One was William Marbury, named a justice of the peace for the District of Columbia. His commission was signed and sealed, but in the last-minute rush the Secretary of State's office failed to deliver it. That Secretary of State was John Marshall, who had just become Chief Justice.\n\nWhen Jefferson took office, his Secretary of State, James Madison, refused to hand it over. Marbury sued directly in the Supreme Court, seeking a writ of mandamus, an order commanding an official to do his legal duty."],
    ["Winning by losing", "Marshall found that Marbury was owed his job, but that the Court could not order it, because the law was unconstitutional.",
      "The case was a trap. If Marshall ordered Madison to deliver the commission, the administration would probably ignore him, exposing the young Court as powerless. If he backed down, he would seem to concede that the executive could ignore the law.\n\nIn February 1803, Marshall ruled that Marbury had a right to his commission and the law should provide a remedy. But the Supreme Court could not provide it. The section of the Judiciary Act that let Marbury come directly to the Court tried to expand its original jurisdiction beyond what the Constitution allowed. When an ordinary law conflicts with the Constitution, the Constitution must win. So Marbury lost, and Jefferson got his result. But the Court had claimed something far bigger: the power to declare acts of Congress unconstitutional."],
    ["Judges say the law", "Judicial review is the power of courts to strike down laws that breach the constitution.",
      "Marshall's reasoning was straightforward. A written constitution is the supreme law, deliberately placed above ordinary laws. If Congress could pass any law regardless of the Constitution, its limits would be meaningless. Someone must decide whether a law conflicts with it, and Marshall argued that this job belonged to judges. In one of the most quoted lines in American law, he wrote that it is emphatically the province and duty of the judicial department to say what the law is.\n\nThis is judicial review. The Constitution had not spelled it out clearly, though some founders had discussed the idea. Marshall made it settled doctrine, using a case where his ruling gave his political opponents a short-term win."],
    ["A sleeping giant", "The Court did not strike down another federal law for over half a century, a reminder that judicial review can be used badly.",
      "For such an important power, judicial review was used sparingly at first. After Marbury, the Supreme Court did not strike down another act of Congress until 1857, in the infamous Dred Scott decision. It held that Black Americans could not be citizens and helped push the country towards civil war. Judicial review is a tool, and courts can use it badly as well as wisely.\n\nOver time the power grew. In the 20th century, the Court struck down laws on everything from segregated schools to campaign spending. Because judges are unelected, debates continue about how boldly they should use this power, and when they should defer to elected lawmakers. Those debates are as old as the decision itself."],
    ["Down under and beyond", "Australia's High Court has long used judicial review, while British courts traditionally cannot strike down Acts of Parliament.",
      "The idea spread to many countries with written constitutions. Australia's framers built a federal system in which the Commonwealth's powers are limited, so it was taken for granted that the High Court would decide whether laws stayed within those limits. In the 1951 Communist Party case, Justice Fullagar wrote that in our system the principle of Marbury v Madison is accepted as axiomatic, meaning self-evident.\n\nThe United Kingdom is different. Under parliamentary sovereignty, British courts cannot strike down an Act of Parliament. Since the Human Rights Act 1998 they can declare a law incompatible with protected rights, but Parliament decides whether to change it. Different systems, different answers to the question of who has the final word."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why was Marshall in a political trap?", ["He had already promised Jefferson a win in return","Ordering it risked defiance; refusing risked looking weak","Congress had banned the Court from hearing such cases","He had no real way of reading the Constitution"], 1, "Either choice risked weakening the young Court."],
    [1, "How did Marshall's ruling lose the case but win something bigger?", ["Marbury lost, but the Court gained the power to void laws","Marbury won, and Jefferson was ordered to resign","The Court claimed the power to appoint all federal judges","The Court gave Congress more power over the judges"], 0, "By striking down a law, the Court established that it could."],
    [2, "What is judicial review?", ["The power of judges to write new laws for Parliament","A parliamentary review of a judge's conduct","Courts striking down laws that breach the constitution","The right of a losing party to appeal to a higher court"], 2, "A constitution's limits mean little unless someone can enforce them."],
    [3, "What does the Dred Scott decision show about judicial review?", ["Courts should never use it","It is only ever used to protect rights","It was used often before 1850","It can be used badly as well as wisely"], 3, "Judicial review depends on how judges choose to use it."],
    [4, "How do British courts differ from Australia's High Court on striking down laws?", ["They strike down laws more often than the High Court does","They cannot strike down Acts, only declare them incompatible","They cannot interpret statutes passed by Parliament at all","They answer to Australia's High Court on constitutional matters"], 1, "Parliament keeps the final word in the United Kingdom."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A letter that never arrived gave judges the power to strike down laws."});

WB.L("common-law-civil-law", "society", "intermediate", "Common law and civil law: the world's two great legal families",
  "Most of the world's legal systems descend from one of two ancestors: medieval English judges, or the law books of ancient Rome. Which one you live under shapes how courts work.",
  [
    ["Two family trees", "Most legal systems descend from either the common law or the civil law tradition, and 'civil law' has two meanings.",
      "Arrested in Sydney, Paris, Tokyo or Chicago, you would meet courtrooms that work differently. Much of the difference is history. Common law grew from medieval England and spread with the British Empire, to Australia, the United States, Canada, New Zealand and India. Civil law grew from Roman law, revived in medieval Europe and organised into national codes. It covers most of continental Europe, Latin America and much of Asia and Africa, so by number of countries it is more widespread.\n\nBeware the vocabulary. In everyday Australian use, civil law means non-criminal disputes, such as contracts and negligence. Here it names a whole tradition built on written codes, and civil law countries have criminal cases too. Lawyers often say 'civilian systems' for clarity."],
    ["Judges on horseback", "The common law grew case by case from the rulings of royal judges, bound by precedent.",
      "After the Norman conquest of 1066, English kings gradually built a national system of royal courts. Under Henry II, who reigned from 1154 to 1189, royal judges travelled on circuits around the country, hearing cases in the king's name. Over time they developed rules that applied across the whole realm, a law common to all of England, which is where the name comes from.\n\nRather than starting from a grand written code, the common law grew case by case. Judges looked at how earlier cases had been decided and followed them. This principle, called precedent, means a decision of a higher court binds lower courts in similar cases. Parliament can override judge-made law with statutes, but much of the law began in courtrooms."],
    ["Rome's second life", "Civil law grew from Justinian's law books, rediscovered in medieval Italy and later turned into national codes.",
      "In the 530s, the Eastern Roman emperor Justinian ordered his lawyers to collect and organise centuries of Roman law. The result, later called the Corpus Juris Civilis, or Body of Civil Law, was a vast compilation of legal rules and expert opinions. For centuries it was largely forgotten in Western Europe.\n\nThen, in the late 1000s, scholars in Bologna, Italy, began studying it closely, and Roman law became the basis of university legal education across Europe. Many centuries later, nations turned these ideas into tidy national codes. The most influential was France's Civil Code of 1804, often called the Napoleonic Code. Germany's Civil Code of 1900 was another giant, later influencing countries as different as Japan and Greece."],
    ["Inside the courtroom", "Common law trials are usually adversarial, while civil law trials are more inquisitorial, with judges leading the inquiry.",
      "The traditions also differ in how trials work. Common law systems are usually adversarial. Two sides, each with its own lawyers, present evidence and question witnesses, while a judge acts like an umpire and, in serious criminal cases, a jury often decides the facts.\n\nCivil law systems are usually more inquisitorial. Judges, sometimes including an investigating judge before trial, take a more active role in gathering evidence and questioning witnesses, and written files matter more. Juries are less common, although some countries use lay judges who sit alongside professionals. Judges in civil law countries are often career officials trained for the role, rather than experienced lawyers appointed later in life, as is common in Australia."],
    ["Blended and borrowed", "Many places mix traditions, and the two families have grown closer over time.",
      "Real legal systems are rarely pure. Scotland and South Africa have mixed systems combining civil law roots with common law influences. In Canada, Quebec uses a civil code for private law, while the other provinces follow common law. Louisiana's private law shows its French and Spanish heritage. Many countries also blend in religious or customary law.\n\nThe two great families have also drifted closer. Common law countries now rely on huge volumes of statutes, so much of their law is written down much like a code. Meanwhile, courts in civil law countries often follow earlier decisions of higher courts in practice, even where they are not formally bound. Two old family trees, with branches increasingly intertwined."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does 'civil law' mean in the phrase 'civil law tradition'?", ["A legal tradition built on Roman law and written codes","The law dealing only with contracts and negligence","The law governing a country's public servants","Law developed case by case by royal judges"], 0, "In this sense civil law is a whole legal family, and it includes criminal law too."],
    [1, "What is precedent in the common law?", ["A judge's personal preference in each case","A statute passed by Parliament to override judges","A higher court's ruling that lower courts must follow","A rule that each case starts from a written code"], 2, "Judges follow how earlier cases were decided, which is how the common law grew."],
    [2, "Where does civil law come from?", ["Royal judges travelling circuits around medieval England","Roman law compiled under Justinian and later turned into codes","A single treaty signed by the states of medieval Europe","The customs and administration of the British Empire"], 1, "The Corpus Juris Civilis, rediscovered in Bologna, shaped the codes of France and Germany."],
    [3, "How do common law and civil law trials typically differ?", ["Common law lacks lawyers, while civil law always uses them","Civil law relies on juries, common law on career judges","They are identical in how trials are run and who leads","Common law is adversarial, civil law more inquisitorial"], 3, "The traditions differ in who drives the search for evidence."],
    [4, "How have the two traditions changed over time?", ["They have drawn closer, as each borrows from the other","They have grown further apart over the past century","One has completely replaced the other around the world","They no longer share any features at all"], 0, "Common law is increasingly codified, while civil law courts follow precedent in practice."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Two ancient legal families decide how most of the world's courts work. Which is yours?"});

WB.L("communist-party-case", "society", "advanced", "The Communist Party case: when the High Court said no",
  "In 1950, at the height of the Cold War, the Australian Parliament banned the Communist Party. Within months, the High Court struck the law down, in one of its most important decisions.",
  [
    ["A Cold War law", "In 1950 the Menzies government passed a law to dissolve the Communist Party of Australia, and it was challenged almost at once.",
      "By 1950 the Cold War was deepening. The Soviet Union had tested an atomic bomb, communists had won China's civil war, and the Korean War began in June, with Australian forces soon involved. At home, a major coal strike in 1949 sharpened fears of communist influence in the unions.\n\nRobert Menzies' Liberal and Country Party coalition won the 1949 election promising to ban the Communist Party, then a legal party. Parliament passed the Communist Party Dissolution Act in 1950. Labor, which controlled the Senate, was divided but let it pass. Almost as soon as the act took effect in October, it was challenged in the High Court."],
    ["A sweeping act", "The act dissolved the party, let the government declare other groups and people unlawful, and relied on Parliament's own statements of fact.",
      "The act declared the Communist Party dissolved and its property forfeited to the Commonwealth. On government advice, the Governor-General could also declare other organisations unlawful if they were linked to communism and judged a threat to security or defence. Individuals could be declared too, which barred them from Commonwealth jobs and from office in certain unions.\n\nCrucially, the act opened with recitals: statements in which Parliament itself declared that communists were working to overthrow the government by violence, and that the law was needed for defence. People who were declared had only limited ability to challenge the government's conclusions in court."],
    ["A stream and its source", "Six of the seven judges held that Parliament cannot make its own law valid by declaring the facts that would justify it.",
      "The Communist Party and several unions argued the act exceeded federal power. Dr H. V. Evatt, Labor's deputy leader and a former High Court judge, appeared for the Waterside Workers' Federation, a controversial choice. The Commonwealth relied mainly on the defence power in section 51 of the Constitution. The central question was whether Parliament could simply declare a threat to exist, and so give itself the power to act.\n\nIn March 1951 the High Court struck the act down by six votes to one, with Chief Justice Latham dissenting. Whether a law falls within a head of power is for the courts to decide. As Justice Fullagar put it, a stream cannot rise higher than its source. Australia was not formally at war, so the defence power could not support such measures."],
    ["The rule of law assumed", "Justice Dixon wrote that the Constitution assumes the rule of law, so the limits on government power are for judges to decide.",
      "The most celebrated judgment came from Justice Owen Dixon, later Chief Justice and widely regarded as one of Australia's greatest judges. He observed that history, and not only ancient history, shows that governments as well as private groups can undermine democratic institutions. He added that the Constitution was framed on traditional conceptions, some of which it simply assumes, and that the rule of law is one of them.\n\nIn other words, the Constitution takes for granted that government is subject to law, enforced by independent courts. Dixon's words are quoted in many later cases. They help explain why Australian courts insist that judges, not ministers, set the limits of government power."],
    ["The voters decide", "After the court said no, voters narrowly refused to give Parliament the power, a second check on government.",
      "The Menzies government took the question to the people. After winning a double dissolution election in April 1951, it held a referendum in September asking voters to give the Commonwealth power over communists and communism. Evatt, by then Labor leader, campaigned against it as a threat to civil liberties. Early polls favoured Yes, but support fell during the campaign.\n\nJust under half of voters said Yes, and only Queensland, Western Australia and Tasmania carried it, short of the double majority required. The Communist Party stayed legal, never became a major electoral force, and dissolved itself in the early 1990s.\n\nThe case is still cited when courts weigh security laws, because the same question returns: who decides the limits of government?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the main aim of the Communist Party Dissolution Act of 1950?", ["To ban strikes in the coal industry","To dissolve the Communist Party of Australia","To bring Australia into the Korean War","To give the Senate new powers"], 1, "The Menzies government wanted to ban the then-legal Communist Party, driven by Cold War fears."],
    [1, "What role did the 'recitals' play in the act?", ["Parliament's own declarations that communists were a threat","They listed the penalties for joining the party and its unions","They gave the High Court special powers to review the act","They named every party member who would be declared"], 0, "The recitals tried to make the law valid by having Parliament declare the facts that justified it."],
    [2, "What did Fullagar mean by 'a stream cannot rise higher than its source'?", ["Courts must defer to Parliament on matters of security","Parliament cannot create its own power by declaring facts","Federal laws always override conflicting state laws","Every law must pass both houses in a fixed order"], 1, "Whether a law falls within Parliament's powers is for the courts to decide, not for Parliament to declare."],
    [3, "What did Justice Dixon say the Constitution assumes?", ["That Parliament is supreme over the courts","That emergency powers may be used in peacetime","That political parties are protected","That government is subject to the rule of law"], 3, "The Constitution takes for granted that government must obey the law, enforced by independent courts."],
    [4, "What happened at the 1951 referendum on communism?", ["It passed in every state of the Commonwealth","It was cancelled after the High Court ruling","It narrowly failed, carrying only three states","It passed, but the government never used it"], 2, "Voters acted as a second check, refusing the power the courts had said Parliament lacked."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Parliament banned a political party, then six judges said it couldn't. Why?"});

